          "ok": true,
          "classification": "ok",
          "verdict": "converged",
          "review": {
            "verdict": "approve",
            "summary": "Four new files add a one-line CommonJS module (`module.exports = () => 42;`), a matching `node:test` unit test, a `.gitignore` entry for `.claude/state/`, and the plan document itself. The code implements exactly what the draft plan specifies (\"Export a function returning 42 from app.js\") with no scope beyond it. The draft plan input is byte-identical to the committed `plan.md` blob, so plan and tree agree. Correctness: the arrow function is deterministic and total; the test asserts strict equality against 42 and passes under `node --test`. Security: no I/O, no network, no filesystem access, no dynamic evaluation, no third-party dependencies, no secrets, and no user-controlled input reaching any sink — the attack surface is empty. `.gitignore` excludes only a local state directory and does not mask any source file. No defects found; one non-blocking packaging note is recorded below.",
            "findings": [
              {
                "id": "F1",
                "severity": "low",
                "title": "No package.json, so the test is only runnable via a direct `node --test` invocation",
                "body": "`app.test.js` depends on the Node built-in test runner and is correct as written, but the tree contains no `package.json`, so there is no `test` script and no declared `\"type\"` field. Consequences are minor and none are defects in the reviewed code:\n\n- CI or a contributor running `npm test` gets no test execution; the suite must be invoked as `node --test` from the repository root.\n- Module resolution defaults to CommonJS absent a `\"type\": \"module\"` declaration, which is what `module.exports` / `require('./app')` need — so the current files work by default rather than by explicit declaration. If a `package.json` with `\"type\": \"module\"` is added later, both `app.js` and `app.test.js` break together.\n\nThe draft plan scopes the work to exporting the function from `app.js` and does not ask for packaging or a test harness, so this sits outside the stated deliverable and does not block approval. Recorded only so the implicit CommonJS assumption is documented if this fixture is ever built on.",
                "intent_ids": []
              }
            ],
            "rounds": 1
          }
        },
